---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/") ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL : import.meta.env.BASE_URL + "/";
---
<Layout title="AI & Decision Making Research — Rahul Jain">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h1 class="page-title">AI &amp; Decision Making Research</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">
      My starting premise is that human intelligence is remarkably flexible and general in ways today's
      algorithms aren't. I use reinforcement-learning agents as a controllable baseline — not the
      reference class humans should be measured against, but a comparison point that helps isolate what's
      actually distinctive about how humans learn, generalize, and make decisions.
    </p>

    <section>
      <div class="subsection">
        <h2 class="subsection-title">Research: <a href="https://alleninstitute.org/person/stefan-mihalas" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Allen Institute (Mihalas Lab)<Icon name="external" /></a></h2>
        <div class="entry-head" style="margin-bottom:4px;">
          <div><span class="entry-role">Research Collaborator</span></div>
          <div class="entry-date">Jul 2026 – Present</div>
        </div>
        <p class="blurb">
          Continuing the same thread from Hexxed and my senior thesis, now on the mechanistic side: I'm
          contributing to a computational framework unifying biologically-inspired predictive neural
          networks with attention-based memory mechanisms, studying how networks encode and retrieve
          working memory over time. Specifically, I'm looking at how heterogeneous per-synapse learning
          rates — different parts of a network learning at different speeds, closer to how biological
          synapses work — change what a network retains and prioritizes from recent experience, and
          whether that gets a model any closer to the flexibility biological memory shows. The
          theoretical predictions have been validated across <strong>16 complex sequential decision-making tasks</strong>,
          and a paper is in preparation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="subsection">
        <h2 class="subsection-title">Senior Thesis &amp; Hexxed</h2>
        <p class="blurb">
          My senior thesis, <em>Evaluating Reinforcement Learning Agents in Hexxed Game Using Neuro-Nav
          Toolkit</em> asks whether reinforcement-learning
          agents can serve as a meaningful baseline for understanding how humans solve genuinely hard
          problems. Hexxed — a mobile strategy game built in-house at the W.M. Keck Science Center — is
          structurally a version of the <strong>Knapsack Problem, a classic NP-hard optimization task</strong>. Using data
          from <strong>10,000+ players and 2.5 million+ in-game actions</strong>, three consistent patterns emerged in how
          humans approached it:
        </p>
        <dl class="skills-grid" style="margin-top:16px;">
          <dt>Picky</dt>
          <dd>Players relied on a narrow, preferred set of strategies rather than exploring the full space of options — a simplification that speeds up decisions but can miss better solutions.</dd>
          <dt>Sticky</dt>
          <dd>Players kept repeating a strategy well past the point of diminishing returns — and, counter to the obvious assumption, that persistence wasn't a sign of poor performance. Players who stuck with a strategy on their first attempts were more likely to beat the level quickly; the less successful players were the ones more willing to abandon a strategy and explore.</dd>
          <dt>Leaps of insight</dt>
          <dd>Players occasionally jumped from a low-reward strategy straight to a high-reward one with no gradual ramp — a discontinuity that doesn't show up in agents that update incrementally.</dd>
        </dl>
        <p class="blurb" style="margin-top:16px;">
          To compare this against machine learning, I represented Hexxed as a 44-state Markov Decision
          Process using the open-source <a href="https://github.com/awjuliani/neuro-nav" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Neuro-Nav Toolkit<Icon name="external" /></a>, then ran three classic reinforcement-learning
          algorithms against it — TD-Q, Dyna-Q, and SARSA — each representing a different family of
          learning strategy (pure trial-and-error, model-based simulation, and on-policy learning,
          respectively). Dyna-Q, which builds and simulates from an internal model of the environment,
          <strong>came closest to human performance</strong> — plausibly because it's the most structurally similar to how
          humans seem to build and test mental models. But none of the three reproduced the sudden leaps
          of insight or the immediate coupling between early persistence and success that showed up in the
          human data — evidence that whatever humans are doing to solve a task like this <strong>isn't just a
          faster version of the same optimization process</strong>.
        </p>
        <p class="blurb" style="margin-top:16px;">
          I presented these findings at the <a href="https://www.neuromonster.org/nm2023" target="_blank" rel="noopener">International Symposium on the Mathematics of Neuroscience<Icon name="external" /></a> in
          Rhodes, Greece, and again at the <a href="https://consciousness.arizona.edu/science-consciousness-conference" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Science of Consciousness Conference<Icon name="external" /></a>.
        </p>
        <p class="blurb" style="margin-top:8px;">
          <a href={base + "files/hexxed-senior-thesis-excerpt.pdf"} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read an excerpt of the thesis (PDF)<Icon name="external" /></a>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="subsection">
        <h2 class="subsection-title">On AI and Cognitive Agency</h2>
        <p class="blurb">
          All of this feeds into how I think about AI product design. If people learn through <strong>productive
          struggle</strong> — narrowing their own options, testing bad ideas, occasionally leaping to insight —
          then an AI system that removes all friction from a task doesn't just save time, it can
          short-circuit the process that builds real understanding. I'm interested in how AI "thought
          partners" can be designed to collaborate with users in a way that sharpens judgment rather than
          substituting for it, drawing on the <a href="https://doi.org/10.3758/BF03193814" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ash &amp; Wiley impasse-restructuring mechanism<Icon name="external" /></a> and recent
          work connecting productive struggle to durable learning (Collins et al., 2024, <em>Nature Human
          Behaviour</em>). The same question sits underneath a much more mundane project like Mizen: even a
          recipe-extraction pipeline is an engineering answer to a version of the same problem — how do you
          retrieve the right knowledge from a noisy source and represent it usefully downstream, the way a
          person does almost without thinking.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="subsection">
        <h2 class="subsection-title">Publications &amp; Presentations</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Jain, R., et al. Working paper unifying predictive neural networks and attention-based memory mechanisms, Allen Institute (Mihalas Lab), in preparation.</li>
          <li>Jain, R., Agarwal, G., and Osborn, J. <a href={base + "files/hexxed-senior-thesis-excerpt.pdf"} target="_blank" rel="noopener">"Evaluating Reinforcement Learning Agents in Hexxed Game Using Neuro-Nav Toolkit."<Icon name="external" /></a> Pomona College Senior Thesis, April 2024.</li>
          <li>Jain, R. "Leaps of Insight in Humans Learning a Complex Skill." <a href="https://consciousness.arizona.edu/science-consciousness-conference" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Science of Consciousness Conference<Icon name="external" /></a>, April 2024.</li>
          <li>Jain, R. "You Got Hexxed: Persistence during Complex Skill Learning." <a href="https://www.neuromonster.org/nm2023" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>International Symposium on the Mathematics of Neuroscience</em><Icon name="external" /></a>, Rhodes, Greece, 2023.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <img class="page-photo" src={base + "images/rahul-hexxed-talk.jpg"} alt="Rahul Jain presenting Hexxed research at the International Symposium on the Mathematics of Neuroscience, Rhodes, Greece" width="900" height="501" style="max-width:600px;" />
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
